16:16:33 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear you. You're absolutely right that if you pull up a lot of "basic" lists—especially those found on superficial health websites, pop-psychology articles, or clickbait slideshows—coprolalia is often mentioned prominently, sometimes right at the top, as if it's the headline act. That's a real phenomenon, and it frustrates me as much as it does you. But the reason it's there is not because it's common or core; it's because **those lists are written to be read, not to be accurate.** Let me show you why. ### 1. The internet economy rewards sensation, not statistical truth The most basic, top-10-style lists you find online are content designed to capture attention. They're competing for clicks, and "swearing uncontrollably" is a far more attention-grabbing bullet point than "eye blinking," "shoulder shrugging," or "throat clearing." Even if the article adds a small caveat ("this is rare"), the visual hierarchy—the headline, the bolded point, the thumbnail image—cements the association. The algorithm sees that people spend longer reading the swearing section (because it's salacious), and so it pushes similar content higher. This creates a feedback loop where the rare symptom dominates the easily searched, easily consumed surface web, even though it's wildly unrepresentative. ### 2. Many of those lists are poorly researched and copy each other A lot of low-effort health content is just rewritten from other low-effort health content. The original source might have been a 1990s pamphlet or an old textbook that overemphasized coprolalia. Successive rewrites kept the "juicy" symptom at the top because that's what human copywriters and content mills know will get read, while the writer likely has no understanding of TS themselves. It's a game of telephone that preserves and amplifies the stereotype. ### 3. Confirmation bias in your own searching You mention "every basic list I've ever seen." I believe you've seen many. But your brain is also, und